   "text": "A home Wi-Fi network carries a video stream from the internet to a laptop through a series of steps involving a router, access point, radio channels, and packet transmission. Here's a simplified explanation:\n\n1. **Router and Access Point**: The router is the central hub of your Wi-Fi network. It connects to the internet and broadcasts a wireless signal that other devices can receive. An access point is a type of router that is specifically designed to provide a wireless network for a single device or a small group of devices. Both routers and access points use radio waves to transmit and receive data.\n\n2. **Radio Channels**: Wi-Fi operates on radio frequencies, which are divided into channels. Each channel can carry data at a different speed and is used by different devices. The router and access point use these channels to transmit data to and from the internet and to other devices on the network.\n\n3. **Packet Transmission**: When you send a video stream, it is broken down into smaller units called packets. Each packet contains a small part of the video data and is sent over the network. The router and access point receive these packets and reassemble them into the complete video stream when they reach the laptop.\n\n4. **Transmission Over Radio Waves**: The router and access point use radio waves"
